            <ArticleTitle>Leisure physical activity and quality of life after coronary artery bypass graft surgery for patients with metabolic syndrome in Taiwan.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>410-20</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.hrtlng.2009.10.004</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The purpose of this correlational study was to explore the physical activity and quality of life of postcoronary artery bypass graft patients with metabolic syndrome.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Seventy patients were recruited via purposive sampling. Data were collected through three instruments: a demographic questionnaire, the Modifiable Activity Questionnaire, and the Medical Outcomes Study Short Form 36-Health Survey. The data were analyzed using SPSS software, version 17.0 (SPSS, Inc., Chicago, IL), and data analysis included percentages, means, standard deviations, correlation coefficients, and forward regressions.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Patients with a higher level of physical activity tended to have better quality of life. Our findings also indicated that the total Modifiable Activity Questionnaire score and age explained 15.1% of the variance in the physical component summary of quality of life, and 13.6% of the variance in the mental component summary of quality of life.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Based on this research, nursing staff should develop physical activity-related interventions to promote quality of life.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>Copyright © 2010 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
